WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.950
"The Batman" is a complicated film with several
narrative threads unraveling at the same time.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:10.790
Some of the story revelations go by so quickly,
they can leave audiences wondering about their

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:11.790
meaning.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:15.570
Keep watching to see the most confusing moments
in "The Batman" explained.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:18.109
Warning: spoilers ahead.

00:00:18.109 --> 00:00:22.320
In interviews promoting the film, star Robert
Pattinson talked about how different Reeves'

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.350
vision for the source material was.

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:29.130
"I just knew there was something radically
different from anything we'd seen in Batman

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:30.130
movies before."

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:33.750
He felt that this new direction was something
viewers would find evident from the film's

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:35.580
shocking opening moments.

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:36.990
He wasn't wrong, either.

00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:41.070
The movie opens with a POV shot someone of
peering through a window of a large home,

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.910
where a small child is playing with his father.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:46.220
But it's initially unclear what we're looking
at.

00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:49.910
It's a jarring but appropriate way for a film
like this to open.

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:53.390
It almost looks as if we're seeing is a young
Bruce Wayne before that fateful night that

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:58.130
his parents were killed, which dovetails nicely
with this child's grief mirroring Bruce's.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:02.971
Instead, it's the Riddler stalking his prey,
Gotham's corrupt Mayor, before going in for

00:01:02.971 --> 00:01:04.010
the kill.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:07.770
The patience to let the scene linger for as
long as it does really sets up the tone and

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.119
pace of the film.

00:01:10.119 --> 00:01:15.149
The first time we see mob boss Carmine Falcone,
it's through Selena's eyes as Bruce monitors

00:01:15.149 --> 00:01:17.710
her infiltration of 44 Below.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:22.369
However, the first time he and Bruce meet
face to face, it's at the mayor's funeral,

00:01:22.369 --> 00:01:26.840
where Falcone remarks that Bruce is the only
person in the city more reclusive than he

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:27.840
is.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:31.850
Falcone tells a brief story about how Thomas
Wayne once saved his life after the mobster

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:32.850
was shot.

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:37.040
He showed up on Wayne's doorstep, with Bruce's
father operating on Falcone on the dining

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:39.320
room table, while Bruce watched from afar.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:41.579
"You don't think that means something?"

00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:42.979
Falcone asks Bruce.

00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:45.939
Bruce says it means his father took the Hippocratic
Oath.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:49.270
When Falcone laughs at this, Bruce doesn't
understand what's so funny.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:52.100
"Hippocratic" sounds to Falcone like "hypocrite."

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:55.909
Even though Bruce thinks his father was a
good man, Falcone remembers the day Thomas

00:01:55.909 --> 00:01:58.219
Wayne asked him to handle a problem for him.

00:01:58.219 --> 00:02:02.560
It resulted in a murder, so he finds it hilarious
that Bruce doesn't know the full scope of

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.860
his own father's complex morality.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:10.610
The revelation that Falcone murdered Selina's
mother that is one of the film's more nuanced

00:02:10.610 --> 00:02:11.610
reveals.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:15.390
When Selina tells her tragic origin story
to Bruce, she mentions that after her mother

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:21.220
was murdered, Falcone let Selina get swallowed
up by Gotham's awful social services system.

00:02:21.220 --> 00:02:25.380
It's not until Annika's message is discovered
on Selina's voicemail that we're able to put

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:27.100
the rest of the pieces together.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:30.830
On the recording, we can hear that Falcone
is the rat they've been searching for and

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.670
that he's the one who personally killed Annika.

00:02:33.670 --> 00:02:36.460
Even worse, he did it with his bare hands
around her neck.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:40.600
Falcone's penchant for strangling the women
who get in his way is confirmed even further

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:45.440
when he tries to kill Selina, trying to choke
her, just like he did to her mother.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:50.590
Once The Riddler has been caught, he is revealed
to be forensic accountant Edward Nashton,

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:54.880
a traumatized orphan who uncovered the rot
at the core of the Gotham Renewal Fund.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.490
It was the corruption of the city that turned
him into a vengeful killer.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:02.870
When rooting through his apartment for more
clues about his motivations, Batman sees that

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.860
he has one last password-protected video to
unlock.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:09.730
Based on Nashton's news clippings, Bruce is
concerned that the Riddler has figured out

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:11.010
who The Batman really is.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:13.270
"I think I'm his last target."

00:03:13.270 --> 00:03:17.980
When Batman arrives at Arkham, Nashton tells
a long story about how he hated Bruce Wayne

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:19.420
while growing up in an orphanage.

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:23.570
To Nashton, it seemed that the only sympathy
Gotham had for lost children was given to

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:25.540
a billionaire kid who lived in a mansion.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:29.760
It feels like Nashton is ramping up to reveal
the truth, but it becomes clear that he's

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.540
not taunting Bruce about knowing his true
identity.

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:36.700
Rather, the Riddler is lamenting the fact
that together, the two of them took down every

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:39.260
one of his targets except Wayne.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.930
Nashton isn't talking to Batman as an adversary
but as a partner.

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:47.330
After all, they're both vengeful, angry men
who suffered in their youth and want to root

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:49.510
out corruption and see justice served.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:54.060
This moment and its implications are the beginning
of the end for Bruce relying entirely on fear

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:55.060
and violence.

00:03:55.060 --> 00:04:00.530
He sees the limits of what he has done, and
worse sees the evil his war on crime has inadvertently

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:03.100
inspired.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:07.640
Another revelation takes place within Arkham's
walls before the final credits begin to roll.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:12.140
As Nashton has a meltdown in his cell knowing
that Batman has thwarted his plans, a friendly

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.040
cell neighbor comforts him.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:18.560
Through their interaction, it becomes pretty
clear that the individual Nashton is befriending

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.219
is supposed to be the Joker.

00:04:20.219 --> 00:04:23.860
However, what's confusing is Barry Keoghan's
casting.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:27.490
Fans assumed that he'd be playing the Joker
in some kind of post-credits scene.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:31.949
This scene taking place before the film's
actual closing moments is a bit of a shock,

00:04:31.949 --> 00:04:35.870
but largely because it begs the question of
how the Joker ended up in Arkham.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:37.039
Is he already the Joker?

00:04:37.039 --> 00:04:39.449
Did Batman catch him in a previous adventure?

00:04:39.449 --> 00:04:44.430
Do we really need another live-action Joker
so soon after Joaquin Phoenix's Oscar-winning

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:46.180
turn with the character?

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:50.090
Only time will tell if he shows up in a potential
sequel.

